    tip: "Picture a line from “no” to “yes”: *can't* at the “no” end, *might, may* and *could* in the middle, *must* at the “yes” end. Before you guess, ask yourself where on the line you are.",
    note: "In American English, *must not* is common for a negative guess, usually from evidence: *He must not be hungry. He hasn't touched his food.* *Can't* sounds stronger, as if the idea is impossible. The short form *mustn't* is rare in American speech.",
